2026-09-03T09:45Z claude created

2026-09-03T09:45Z claude note filed to give one of 0590's three awaiting entries a queue handle; DECISIONS.md keeps the argument, this ticket carries only the decision

2026-09-03T10:10Z claude note THE CONSTANT THIS TICKET ASKS ABOUT DOES NOT EXIST IN CODE, established 2026-09-03 (evidence on 0590's log of the same date). The keyword path issues one MATCH at LIMIT = limit*3 with no scope filter and no deeper refetch, so there is no 4 096 to raise and no ladder to admit a third rung to. The question is not void -- SPEC.md 5.2.6 is a design this repo intends to ship, and ruling on it before it is built is cheaper than after -- but it is a question about a shape rather than a number. THE AUTHOR'S REFRAME, 2026-09-03, which supersedes the brief this ticket was filed with: a query plus one deeper retry is a two-point approximation of a search that should terminate on a CONDITION (k in-scope hits found) rather than on a BUDGET, and the retry discards everything the first pass computed. The shape with no constant in it is a cursor over the ranked stream, stopping at k or at genuine exhaustion, whose work is proportional to how deep the scope actually sat and which cannot give up spuriously. That is the ledger's second candidate, filtering the ranked stream before truncation, reached as the REMOVAL OF A RETRY rather than as new machinery. It is also already the idiom on the other arm: the vector exact scan streams row by row with a running top-K (sqlite-index.ts:1229), so adopting it on the keyword side is consistency, not novelty. WHAT I RECOMMENDED FIRST AND NO LONGER DO: admitting a raised depth as a candidate. Raising a constant treats the symptom of a design that should not have carried one. The ruling worth having is on the shape, before any constant. NOT ESTABLISHED HERE, and it sizes the win rather than deciding it: whether FTS5 must scan all matching rows to compute bm25 regardless of LIMIT, which would make the retry close to pure waste and the cursor strictly dominant. That is one cheap experiment, not an argument.
--- body ---

## The decision

`DECISIONS.md`, awaiting entry of 2026-09-03: *"Whether the keyword ladder's
refetch depth is the answer to the scope give-up, rather than a new mechanism
(raised 2026-09-03, reimagining ticket 0590)."* That entry owns the argument and
the evidence; this ticket exists so the decision is visible in the queue rather
than only in a 350 kB ledger.

In one line: SPEC.md §5.2.6's refetch depth is a **constant**, not a measured
bound. The 2026-09-02 ruling named two candidate replacements should partial
answers prove common — an indexed temporary table, and filtering the ranked
stream before truncation — and both are mechanisms. Raising the constant is
neither, and it is cheaper than both.

## What is being asked

Not a number. Whether raising the depth is **admitted as a candidate** beside
the two the ledger already names, so that 0590's depth sweep can be acted on
when it lands.

- **Admitted** — 0590's fill-rate-and-latency curve over depth is decisive on
  its own, and a ruling on the constant can follow directly from it.
- **Not admitted** — the sweep still runs (it prices the remainder either way),
  but its evidence returns here for a second round before anything moves.

Moving the constant is a SPEC.md change and stays a ruling whichever way this
goes; 0590 prices it and does not make it.

## Relevant files

- `DECISIONS.md` — the awaiting entry. Read, not edited by this ticket.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.6 — where the constant lives and where any change lands.
- `tickets/0590-measure-scoped-queries-that-fail-to-fill.erg` — the sweep.

## Why 0590 is not `Blocked-by` this

0590's sweep is defined to run at every depth up to the unconstrained ceiling
regardless of the ruling, so a `Blocked-by` would make a schedulable
measurement unschedulable to protect a decision the measurement does not need.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Ruled in `DECISIONS.md`: the depth is, or is not, an admitted candidate.
- [ ] If admitted, 0590 action 5's candidate list names it beside the other two.
